records.filter(r=>r.sourceFile==='four.txt')[2].issues.push('Source reverses GC marking: reachable live objects are marked; unreachable unmarked objects are swept.');
records.filter(r=>r.sourceFile==='four.txt')[0].issues.push('Convention over configuration is chiefly a Rails framework philosophy, not a core Ruby grammar rule.');
records.filter(r=>r.sourceFile==='four.txt')[6].issues.push('Mixins compose module behavior; Ruby still has one superclass per class.');
const fifth=[
 ['Modules provide namespaces and reusable mixed-in behavior. Ruby has single superclass inheritance even when multiple module methods are composed.','module Logging\n  def log(message); puts message; end\nend\nclass Service; include Logging; end',['Source calls mixins "multiple inheritance"; qualify that this is not multiple superclasses.']],
 ['A class can construct instances and inherit one superclass; a module supplies namespaces/mixins and is not directly instantiated as a domain object.','p Class < Module # true\nmodule Labels; end',['Module objects can maintain state; methods mixed into receivers can access receiver instance variables.']],
 ['include adds module methods to a class’s instance lookup chain; extend adds them to one object’s singleton class, which is how it can create class methods.','module Hello\n  def hi; :hi; end\nend\nobj = Object.new; obj.extend(Hello)\np obj.hi'],
 ['public methods can be called normally; private methods are for receiver-internal calls; protected methods permit compatible receiver calls from class-family contexts.','class Person\n  def initialize(age); @age = age; end\n  def older_than?(other); age > other.age; end\n  protected\n  def age; @age; end\nend\np Person.new(30).older_than?(Person.new(20))',['Modern Ruby permits a literal self receiver for private calls; arbitrary explicit receivers remain disallowed. Protected is not simply "available to subclasses"; explain receiver context.']],
 ['A Proc is a callable closure object capturing local bindings and self. Like a JavaScript closure it retains its environment, although JavaScript this rules differ by function form.','class Maker\n  def initialize; @value = 42; end\n  def callback; proc { [self, @value] }; end\nend\np Maker.new.callback.call',['Source incorrectly says Ruby procs do not capture self. instance_exec can deliberately change execution self.']],
 ['Use begin/rescue for expected error types and ensure for resource cleanup. Bare rescue catches StandardError.','begin\n  Integer("bad")\nrescue ArgumentError => e\n  warn e.message\nend'],
 ['Rescue StandardError or narrower application errors so process-control/system exceptions can propagate, including Interrupt and SystemExit.','begin\n  raise ArgumentError, "invalid input"\nrescue StandardError => e\n  warn e.message\nend',['Rescuing Exception catches fatal/control exceptions and can prevent expected shutdown.']],
 ['and/or have lower precedence than assignment; &&/|| have higher precedence. a = nil and true assigns nil, and the expression evaluates to nil.','a = nil and true\np a # nil\nb = true and false\nc = true && false\np [b,c] # [true,false]',['Source reverses the relative precedence of both operator groups. Both groups short-circuit and return operands, not necessarily booleans.']],
 ['Ruby conditionals treat only nil and false as falsey, including in Rails. Therefore a = 0 || 1 assigns and evaluates to 0.','a = 0 || 1\np a # 0',['Rails blank? is a separate predicate: empty/whitespace values can be truthy while blank? is true.']],
 ['method_missing intercepts failed method lookup; implement respond_to_missing? consistently and delegate unknown methods to super.','class Dynamic\n  def method_missing(name, *args, **kwargs, &block)\n    return 42 if name == :answer && args.empty? && kwargs.empty?\n    super\n  end\n  def respond_to_missing?(name, include_private = false)\n    name == :answer || super\n  end\nend\np Dynamic.new.answer',['Source only says "Poor performance" and does not supply an answer. Dynamic dispatch can cost overhead, but correctness/introspection are the first considerations.']],
 ['A domain-specific language expresses a focused problem domain. Ruby’s blocks, flexible method-call syntax and objects allow readable internal DSLs.','class JobPlan\n  attr_reader :steps\n  def initialize; @steps = []; end\n  def step(name); @steps << name; end\nend\np = JobPlan.new; p.step(:import); puts p.steps',['Prefer constrained methods over eval of untrusted strings.']],
 ['RubyGems distributes Ruby libraries/tools as versioned packages. Choose gems by maintenance, API fit, security and compatibility; a favorite should be explained with a real project example.','gem list\n# In Gemfile: gem "rake"\n# Then: bundle install',['An interview preference question has no single universally correct favorite.']],
 ['Rails is strong at convention-driven database web applications and APIs. Workloads demanding tiny runtimes, hard real-time behavior or specialist numerical kernels need separate evaluation.','# Scenario: Rails owns an order API; specialized CPU-intensive processing runs in a measured worker component.',['This is an architectural recommendation with tradeoffs, not a guarantee that Rails cannot handle a particular scale.']],
 ['Choose a concrete maintainability or performance issue and explain a measured improvement, its tradeoffs and backwards compatibility. There is no canonical list of changes to Rails.','# Example interview discussion: replace a callback-heavy purchase workflow with an explicit transactional service and reliable event delivery.',['Distinguish personal preference from language/framework facts.']],
 ['Active Support extends Ruby with helpers such as durations and blank?/present?. Some historical Rails additions, including Hash#slice, later became core Ruby.','require "active_support/all"\np 3.seconds\np({a: 1, b: 2}.slice(:a))',['Hash#slice exists in Ruby 2.5+ without Rails; do not claim it is always Rails-only.']]
];fifth.forEach((a,i)=>{let r=edit('five.txt',i+1,...a);r.explanation=r.directAnswer+' '+r.importantPoints.join(' ');if([4,5,8,10,11,12,13,14,15].includes(i+1))r.issues.push('Original incomplete/outdated claim corrected: '+r.directAnswer);});
// The 30 source HTML articles include many illustrative subsections; sourceAnswer retains all of them.
[[1,1],[2,9],[3,32],[4,22],[5,28],[6,4],[9,3],[11,2],[15,35],[18,37],[20,8],[21,2],[27,64],[29,66]].forEach(([i,j])=>reuse('six.txt',i,j));
const sixSpecific={
2:['puts prints values with line endings and returns nil; print adds no newline and returns nil; p prints inspect forms and returns its argument (or the arguments array for several).','p [1, 2]\nresult = p("hello")\np result','The original phrase "p returns inspect output" is misleading: it prints inspect output but returns the object.'],
4:['Classes create instances and support a superclass; modules provide namespaces and mixed-in methods. A module object can have instance variables, and module methods can call super in an ancestor chain.','module Named\n  @label = "namespace state"\n  def label; @label; end\nend\nclass Person\n  include Named\n  def initialize; @label = "person state"; end\nend\np Person.new.label','Source falsely says modules have no state (except class variables) and cannot use super.'],
6:['Symbols are immutable identifiers; strings are text and may be mutable or frozen. Interning, frozen literals and runtime options make allocation/performance claims context-specific.','p :name.equal?(:name)\ntext = +"name"\ntext << "!"\np text','Dynamic symbols have been garbage-collectable since Ruby 2.2; source table claiming Symbol GC is always No is outdated. Avoiding all string-key hashes is not a valid general rule.'],
7:['map transforms every element, select keeps elements whose block is truthy, and reduce accumulates a result of any type, not necessarily a scalar.','values = [1, 2, 3]\np values.map { |n| n * 2 }\np values.select(&:odd?)\np values.reduce(0, :+)','Without an explicit initial value reduce uses the first element; [].reduce(:+) is nil while [].reduce(0, :+) is 0.'],
8:['nil means no value; false is a boolean false value. blank? is an Active Support predicate for nil/false/empty/whitespace and does not change Ruby truthiness.','require "active_support/core_ext/object/blank"\np [nil.blank?, false.blank?, " ".blank?, 0.blank?] # [true,true,true,false]','blank? is available outside Rails when the relevant Active Support extension is loaded.'],
9:['attr_reader defines a getter, attr_writer defines a setter, and attr_accessor defines both methods backed by an instance variable.','class Person\n  attr_reader :id\n  attr_accessor :name\n  def initialize(id, name); @id, @name = id, name; end\nend\np Person.new(1,"Ana").name','Accessors do not validate type or freeze returned objects; they expose behavior rather than public instance-variable access.'],
10:['Both Ruby and Python can build full-stack or small web applications. Compare Rails/Sinatra with Django/Flask by conventions, integrations, team skills and project constraints.','# Scenario: compare Rails and Django for the same CRUD API with identical database indexes, tests and deployment targets.','The source’s Python-only-lambdas and limited-metaprogramming comparison is false; Python has normal functions, closures, decorators and metaclasses. "Elegant" versus "practical" is subjective.'],
11:['@@variables are shared across an inheritance hierarchy; @variables belong to the current receiver, which may itself be a class object. Neither form is automatically thread-safe.','class Base\n  @count = 0\n  class << self; attr_accessor :count; end\nend\nclass Child < Base; end\np [Base.count, Child.count] # [0,nil]','Class instance variables are not instance variables of constructed objects and are not automatically copied to subclasses. Both forms need synchronization if shared mutable state is accessed concurrently.'],
12:['Rails supplies integrated full-stack conventions; Sinatra supplies a smaller Rack-oriented routing DSL. Either can serve APIs, and application size alone is not a strict rule.','require "sinatra"\nget("/health") { "ok" }','Rails is also Rack-based; the source’s MVC-versus-Rack distinction compares different layers. Its User examples require explicit models, persistence and input validation.'],
13:['nil? checks identity with nil, empty? is defined by collection-like types, and Active Support blank? combines false/nil, emptiness and whitespace semantics.','require "active_support/core_ext/object/blank"\np [" ".nil?, " ".empty?, " ".blank?] # [false,false,true]','nil.empty? raises NoMethodError. blank? can be loaded outside Rails. false.blank? is true, so presence validation is not a boolean-true/false inclusion check.'],
14:['Ruby is class-based and commonly used on servers; JavaScript uses prototypes and runs in browsers and server runtimes. Both support multiple paradigms and asynchronous work through their runtimes/libraries.','puts "Ruby"\n# JavaScript equivalent: console.log("JavaScript");','Ruby can also run in browsers through Wasm, and JavaScript has server/worker concurrency; source table’s absolute client/server and synchronous/asynchronous split is too broad.'],
15:['method_missing handles failed dispatch; respond_to_missing? declares corresponding dynamic capabilities to introspection. Unsupported names must fall through to super.','class Dynamic\n  def method_missing(name, *args, **kwargs, &block)\n    return 42 if name == :answer && args.empty? && kwargs.empty?\n    super\n  end\n  def respond_to_missing?(name, include_private = false)\n    name == :answer || super\n  end\nend\np Dynamic.new.respond_to?(:answer)','The actual hook name includes ?: respond_to_missing?. A broad get_/find_by_ prefix without checking valid fields can misrepresent capabilities.'],
16:['Ruby and PHP can both build web services; Rails, Laravel and WordPress are ecosystems with different goals and conventions. Choose based on requirements and maintainable integration, not subjective syntax rankings.','# Rails API sketch: render json: Product.limit(20).pluck(:id, :name)','Source mass-assignment examples need allowlisted input, authorization, validation handling and correct HTTP statuses before production use.'],
17:['Active Record maps relational rows and associations; Mongoid maps MongoDB documents and supports embedding/references. Both require intentional schema, consistency and indexing design.','# Relational example:\n# class Order < ApplicationRecord; belongs_to :user; end\n# Document example requires Mongoid::Document and configured MongoDB.','MongoDB/Mongoid support multi-document ACID transactions on suitable deployments. "Mongoid means eventual consistency/no migrations" is an inaccurate absolute; document schema changes still need migration/backfill planning.'],
18:['yield invokes a method’s supplied block; call invokes a callable object such as a Proc, lambda or Method. Capture &block when you need to store or forward it explicitly.','def implicit; yield(2); end\ndef explicit(&block); block.call(2); end\np [implicit { |n| n*2 }, explicit { |n| n*2 }]','Do not claim a universal performance difference without benchmarking the Ruby version and workload.'],
19:['Ruby is dynamically typed; Java is statically typed and typically compiled to JVM bytecode then interpreted/JIT-compiled. Both support server development, garbage collection and multiple deployment models.','# Ruby example:\nclass User\n  attr_reader :name\n  def initialize(name); @name = name; end\nend\np User.new("Ana").name','The source’s startup-versus-enterprise and always-faster rankings are unsupported generalizations. Ruby implementations also compile internally and may JIT.'],
20:['require finds and loads a feature once, require_relative resolves relative to the calling file, and load executes the specified file on every invocation.','require "json"\n# Project files: require_relative "lib/user"\n# Reload explicitly: load "lib/user.rb"','require is not restricted to absolute paths; load resolves according to its documented path rules. Rails autoloading is separate.'],
21:['Local, instance (@), class (@@) and global ($) variables differ in ownership/scope; constants begin uppercase. Class-instance variables are ordinary @variables on class objects.','local = 1\n@instance = 2\nVALUE = 3\np [local, @instance, VALUE]','Constants can reference mutable objects and be reassigned with a warning; they are not automatically immutable or universally public across namespaces.'],
22:['Go is statically typed and compiled with goroutines; Ruby is dynamically typed with class-based APIs and a different runtime model. Either can implement web services or microservices.','# Compare representative throughput, memory, latency and development constraints for the same service.','The source’s always-faster/easier and exclusive use-case choices are context-dependent recommendations.'],
23:['Ruby supports while/until/for and iterator methods such as each, times, upto/downto and loop. break exits, next skips ahead and redo restarts the current iteration without rechecking the condition.','[1, 2, 3].each_with_index { |n, i| puts "#{i}: #{n}" }\n3.times { |i| puts i }','for does not introduce a block-local scope the way each’s block does. String interpolation requires double quotes, not the source’s single-quoted examples.'],
24:['Swift is statically typed and compiled, commonly used for Apple platforms and also available elsewhere; Ruby is dynamically typed and commonly used for web/backend tools.','# Swift uses double-quoted strings: let name = "John"\n# Ruby: name = "John"','The source’s Swift example uses invalid single-quoted string syntax. Framework choice and measured constraints matter more than blanket speed/use-case rankings.'],
25:['Use clear interfaces, consistent style, focused tests, locked dependencies, safe input handling and measurement before optimization. Rails-specific MVC/routes are not universal requirements for every Ruby program.','def calculate_sum(numbers)\n  numbers.sum\nend\np calculate_sum([]) # 0','Omitting method-call parentheses is not inherently bad Ruby style. .env is not read by Ruby/Rails automatically without configured tooling; use supported credential/configuration facilities.'],
26:['TypeScript adds compile-time checking to JavaScript and runs after compilation in JavaScript runtimes, including browsers and servers. Ruby remains dynamically typed, with optional external type tooling.','# TypeScript: const name: string = "Ana";\n# Ruby: name = "Ana"','TypeScript is not limited to frontend code, and its types are normally erased at runtime. Ruby is not strictly limited to servers.'],
27:['class_eval/module_eval reopens the receiving class/module context; def inside defines instance methods. instance_eval changes self and def adds singleton methods to that object.','class Person; end\nPerson.class_eval { def greet; "instance"; end }\nPerson.instance_eval { def kind; "class object"; end }\np [Person.new.greet, Person.kind]','The source reverses the usual method-definition distinction and wrongly calls Person.new.instance_method after defining it on Person’s singleton class.'],
28:['Elixir runs on BEAM with immutable data and lightweight isolated processes; Ruby commonly uses mutable objects with threads/fibers/processes. Phoenix and Rails are separate web ecosystems.','# Scenario: Rails handles a CRUD product; a BEAM service owns long-lived concurrent connection state when justified.','Performance depends on workload and runtime; Elixir processes are not OS processes. Single-quoted Elixir literals are charlists rather than ordinary strings.'],
29:['Gems are packages categorized informally by purpose, such as runtime, test, development, adapters and API clients; Bundler groups control where dependencies are used.','source "https://rubygems.org"\ngem "rake"\ngroup :test do\n  gem "rspec"\nend','These are not formal RubyGems package types. Source version pins illustrate syntax and are not a supported-version recommendation. active_support is a require path; the package is activesupport.'],
30:['Follow Rails conventions while keeping domain workflows explicit, input/authorization checks correct, database constraints authoritative and side effects reliable. Profile queries, paginate and test failure paths.','# Application model sketch:\nclass Order < ApplicationRecord\n  after_create_commit :enqueue_receipt\n  private\n  def enqueue_receipt; ReceiptJob.perform_later(id); end\nend','Uniqueness validation needs a database unique constraint for races; callbacks/jobs are not exactly-once delivery. Use authorization-aware cache keys, avoid caching unbounded User.all objects, sanitize LIKE wildcards when needed and protect secrets.']
};
for(const [n,[a,e,issue]]of Object.entries(sixSpecific)){let r=edit('six.txt',+n,a,e,[],issue);r.explanation=a+' '+issue;}
const twelve=[
 ['Encapsulation hides implementation behind APIs; inheritance reuses/substitutes behavior; polymorphism lets different objects satisfy one interface; abstraction exposes the necessary model.','class Invoice\n  def total_cents; 1000; end\nend\nclass Refund\n  def total_cents; -1000; end\nend\np [Invoice.new, Refund.new].sum(&:total_cents)'],
 ['Use explicit dynamic method definition/delegation when repeated structure warrants it; method_missing is a fallback that needs consistent introspection.','class Metric\n  [:count, :sum].each { |name| attr_accessor name }\nend',['Prefer public_send/allowlists for user-selected methods; send and eval are not safe input validation.']],
 ['Rails offers jobs, real-time channels, engines, rich persistence, caching and other facilities. Service objects are an application design pattern, not a built-in Rails subsystem.','# Example app structure: ApplicationJob subclasses for jobs, ApplicationCable channels for real-time messaging.',['Framework/version and deployment backend determine actual behavior; avoid calling every application pattern a framework feature.']],
 ['Design a small explicit object API around the domain, then add blocks or method generation only where it makes usage clearer.','class Plan\n  attr_reader :tasks\n  def initialize; @tasks = []; end\n  def task(name); @tasks << name; end\nend\nplan = Plan.new\nplan.task(:import)',['Do not execute untrusted DSL text with eval; choose clear scope and good error messages.']],
 ['Organize a large Rails application around cohesive responsibilities and stable boundaries; use services/presenters/engines only when they solve a concrete complexity problem.','# Workflow: controller -> explicit checkout service -> models/transaction -> reliable job/event delivery.',['Service objects and engines are recommendations, not mandatory layers; excessive abstraction can increase navigation and coupling.']],
 ['Measure SQL and rows loaded; fix N+1 with suitable preloading, use selective queries/pluck, add indexes and process large data in bounded batches.','User.preload(:posts).find_each { |u| u.posts.each { |p| puts p.title } }',['includes may switch between preloading and joins; select partial models carefully and compare EXPLAIN plans. Bullet detects patterns but does not replace measurement.']],
 ['Ruby polymorphism primarily means shared behavior interfaces/duck typing. Rails polymorphic associations and STI are different persistence modeling tools with specific tradeoffs.','class Email\n  def deliver; :email; end\nend\nclass Sms\n  def deliver; :sms; end\nend\np [Email.new, Sms.new].map(&:deliver)',['Polymorphic associations lack an ordinary single-target foreign key; STI uses one table and a type column, with shared schema/validation consequences.']],
 ['Keep callbacks small and deterministic; use after_commit for actions that depend on a committed row, and explicit services for multi-step business workflows.','class Order < ApplicationRecord\n  after_create_commit :enqueue_receipt\n  private\n  def enqueue_receipt; ReceiptJob.perform_later(id); end\nend',['after_commit does not guarantee external enqueue delivery; critical workflows may need a transactional outbox and idempotent consumer. Callback ordering/version differences should be tested.']],
 ['Rescue expected exception types near a useful recovery boundary; controllers can translate application errors using rescue_from. Log context safely and re-raise unexpected failures.','begin\n  Integer("invalid")\nrescue ArgumentError => e\n  warn e.message\nend',['Avoid Exception-wide rescue, unbounded retries and logging secrets or personal data unnecessarily.']],
 ['Use respond_to?, method, parameters, ancestors and instance_variables to inspect capabilities and dispatch explicitly; reflection should support clear APIs rather than replace them.','p "hello".respond_to?(:upcase)\np "hello".method(:upcase).arity\np String.ancestors',['A method_missing implementation should pair with respond_to_missing?. Treat externally supplied names as untrusted input.']]
];twelve.forEach((a,i)=>{let r=edit('twelve.txt',i+1,...a);r.explanation=r.directAnswer+' '+r.importantPoints.join(' ');});
